Added `capitalize` to `src/str.js:7` and a test in `test/str.test.js:11`. Both tests pass.

```js
function capitalize(s) {
  if (s.length === 0) return s;
  return s[0].toUpperCase() + s.slice(1);
}
```

Two notes on the semantics I picked, since "capitalize" is ambiguous:

- It uppercases the first character and leaves the rest untouched, so `capitalize('aBC')` → `'ABC'`, not `'Abc'`. If you want the tail lowercased, say so and I'll change it.
- `s[0]` indexes by UTF-16 code unit, so a leading astral character (emoji, some CJK extensions) would be split into a lone surrogate. `[...s]` or `s.codePointAt(0)` would handle that. I left it simple to match `trim`'s style — worth fixing only if this library sees non-BMP input.

