Added `reverse` to `src/str.js:12` with a test in `test/str.test.js:17`. All three tests pass.

```js
function reverse(s) {
  return [...s].reverse().join('');
}
```

I used spread rather than `s.split('')` — it iterates by code point, so emoji and other astral characters survive instead of having their surrogate pairs flipped. Same line count, so no reason to take the broken version; the test covers it.

The remaining limitation: this reverses code points, not grapheme clusters. A string with combining marks (`'e\u0301'` → `'́e'`) or a ZWJ emoji sequence still comes out mangled. Fixing that needs `Intl.Segmenter`, which is a real step up in complexity — I'd only reach for it if this library is expected to handle arbitrary user text. Say the word if so.

